.TH BOOTWIRE 1 "2026-11-12" "BootWire 0.2.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
bootwire \- update Cortex-M firmware and send commands over the BootWire protocol
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B bootwire
.RB [ \-p
.IR PORT ]
.RB [ \-b
.IR BAUD ]
.RB [ \-t
.IR SECONDS ]
.RB [ \-r
.IR COUNT ]
.RB [ \-c
.IR FILE ]
.I COMMAND
.RI [ ARGS ...]
.br
.B bootwire
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.B \-i
.br
.B bootwire \-\-help
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B bootwire
is the host side of the BootWire protocol v0.2, a small stop-and-wait protocol
for updating the firmware of a 32-bit ARM Cortex-M microcontroller over UART
and for sending user-defined commands to its bootloader.
.PP
Every invocation opens the port, starts a session with CONNECT, runs one
command and closes the port. The target reports its application region, erase
block size, write alignment and largest payload in the CONNECT response, and
.B bootwire
uses those values; nothing about the flash layout is fixed on the host.
.PP
Each request is protected by a CRC-16 and sent in SLIP framing. When no valid
response arrives in time the same request is sent again, up to the retry limit.
An error status from the target is final and is not retried.
.PP
Global options must come
.I before
the command name.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-port " \fIPORT\fR"
Serial device (for example
.IR /dev/ttyACM0 ),
a pyserial URL (for example
.IR socket://192.168.1.50:4001 ),
or
.B sim
for the built-in simulated target. Without this option and without a
configured port, the only serial port present is used; if there are several,
.B bootwire
asks which one to use.
.TP
.BR \-b ", " \-\-baud " \fIBAUD\fR"
Baud rate. Default: 115200.
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-timeout " \fISECONDS\fR"
Time to wait for a response before resending. Default: 0.5.
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-\-retries " \fICOUNT\fR"
Number of resends after the first attempt. Default: 3.
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-config " \fIFILE\fR"
Read settings from
.I FILE
instead of searching the default locations (see
.BR FILES ).
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-interactive
Choose the command from a menu instead of naming it on the command line.
.TP
.B \-\-install\-completion
Install tab completion for the current shell.
.TP
.B \-\-show\-completion
Print the completion script for the current shell.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Show a summary of options and commands. Each command also accepts
.BR \-\-help .
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B info
Connect and show what the target reports: protocol and bootloader versions,
device ID, application region, erase block size, write alignment and largest
payload.
.TP
.BR ping " [" \-d " \fIDATA\fR] [" \-\-hex "] [" \-n " \fICOUNT\fR]"
Check the link. The target must echo the bytes it receives; the round-trip
time is printed.
.RS
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-data " \fIDATA\fR"
Bytes to echo, as text, or as hexadecimal with
.BR \-\-hex .
Default: the text "ping".
.TP
.B \-\-hex
Read
.I DATA
as hexadecimal bytes, for example "C0 DB".
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-count " \fICOUNT\fR"
Number of pings. Default: 1.
.RE
.TP
.BR erase " [" \-a " \fIADDRESS\fR] [" \-l " \fILENGTH\fR]"
Erase flash. The range is rounded out to whole erase blocks.
.RS
.TP
.BR \-a ", " \-\-address " \fIADDRESS\fR"
Start address. Default: the start of the application region.
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-length " \fILENGTH\fR"
Number of bytes to erase. Default: to the end of the application region.
.RE
.TP
.BR write " \fIFILE\fR [" \-a " \fIADDRESS\fR]"
Write a raw binary image into flash that is already erased. The image is
padded to the write alignment and sent in the largest chunks the target
accepts. The target reads each chunk back after programming it.
.RS
.TP
.BR \-a ", " \-\-address " \fIADDRESS\fR"
Load address. Default: the start of the application region.
.RE
.TP
.BR verify " \fIFILE\fR [" \-a " \fIADDRESS\fR]"
Check that flash holds exactly this image by comparing the CRC-32 of the
padded file with the CRC-32 the target computes over the same range.
.RS
.TP
.BR \-a ", " \-\-address " \fIADDRESS\fR"
Load address. Default: the start of the application region.
.RE
.TP
.B run
Leave the bootloader and start the application. The target checks the
application's vector table first and refuses to jump to an invalid image.
.TP
.BR flash " \fIFILE\fR [" \-a " \fIADDRESS\fR] [" \-\-no\-run ]
Complete update: erase the blocks the image touches, write it, verify it,
then start the application.
.RS
.TP
.BR \-a ", " \-\-address " \fIADDRESS\fR"
Load address. Default: the start of the application region.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-run
Stay in the bootloader after the image has been verified.
.RE
.TP
.BR send " \fICMD\fR [" \-d " \fIDATA\fR] [" \-\-hex ]
Send any command and print the status and payload of the response.
.I CMD
is a command ID from 0x00 to 0x7F; user commands occupy 0x40 to 0x7F.
.RS
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-data " \fIDATA\fR"
Payload, as text, or as hexadecimal with
.BR \-\-hex .
Default: empty.
.TP
.B \-\-hex
Read
.I DATA
as hexadecimal bytes.
.RE
.PP
Addresses, lengths and command IDs accept decimal, or hexadecimal with a
.B 0x
prefix. Image files are raw binaries
.RI ( .bin )
linked for the load address.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
The target refused the command or did not answer.
.TP
.B 2
Bad usage, or a file or port that could not be opened.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B BOOTWIRE_CONFIG
Path of a settings file. Used when
.B \-\-config
is not given.
.SH FILES
Settings are read from the first of these that exists; command-line options
override them.
.PP
.RS
.nf
the file named by \fB\-\-config\fR
the file named by \fBBOOTWIRE_CONFIG\fR
\fI./bootwire.yaml\fR
\fI~/.bootwire.yaml\fR
.fi
.RE
.PP
The file is YAML:
.PP
.RS
.nf
general:
  timeout: 0.5        # seconds to wait for a response
  erase_timeout: 5.0  # seconds to wait for one ERASE response
  max_retries: 3      # resends after the first attempt
serial:
  port:               # empty = automatic; a device, a pyserial URL, or "sim"
  baudrate: 115200
.fi
.RE
.PP
The ERASE timeout can only be set in this file.
.SH EXAMPLES
Show what a board reports:
.PP
.RS
.nf
bootwire \-p /dev/ttyACM0 info
.fi
.RE
.PP
Flash an image and start it:
.PP
.RS
.nf
bootwire \-p /dev/ttyACM0 flash build/app.bin
.fi
.RE
.PP
Flash at a higher baud rate with a longer timeout, and stay in the bootloader:
.PP
.RS
.nf
bootwire \-p /dev/ttyUSB0 \-b 921600 \-t 1.0 \-r 5 flash app.bin \-\-no\-run
.fi
.RE
.PP
Erase one 2 KiB block:
.PP
.RS
.nf
bootwire erase \-a 0x08008000 \-l 0x800
.fi
.RE
.PP
Test the link with bytes that need SLIP escaping:
.PP
.RS
.nf
bootwire ping \-n 10 \-d "C0 DB" \-\-hex
.fi
.RE
.PP
Send a user command with a three-byte payload:
.PP
.RS
.nf
bootwire send 0x40 \-d "01 02 03" \-\-hex
.fi
.RE
.PP
Work without hardware, against the built-in simulated target:
.PP
.RS
.nf
bootwire \-p sim flash app.bin
.fi
.RE
.SH NOTES
The simulated target selected by
.B \-p sim
lives only for one invocation, so each command starts from erased flash. To
keep a simulated target between commands, run
.B python3 \-m bootwire.sim
and pass the pseudo-terminal it prints to
.BR \-p .
.PP
.B run
hands the UART to the application, so the session ends there. Put the board
back into its bootloader before the next command.
.PP
The protocol has no authentication: anyone with access to the UART can flash
any image.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR bootwire\-gui (1)
is the desktop application built on the same library.
.PP
The protocol specification and the library documentation are in the project
repository, under
.I docs/protocol/README.md
and
.IR README.md .
.SH AUTHOR
Mahmoud Abdelraouf.
